{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 30, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a prominent Crusader lord who ruled the County of Tripoli from 1187 to 1189 and again from 1197 until his death in 1202. He was a member of the House of Toulouse and the grandson of Raymond II Count of Tripoli through his mother Hodierna. His father was Prince Bohemond III of Antioch. Raymond III was born around 1140 and succeeded his uncle Raymond II as the Count of Tripoli. His tenure was marked by struggles against the Ayyubid dynasty under Saladin who was steadily gaining control over much of the Levant during this period.\n\n*highlighted section part 1: Early Life and Succession*\nBefore inheriting the County of Tripoli Raymond III had already established himself in the region. He was a knight and a participant in the Crusades. In 1165 he was married to Euphemia of Grandson a daughter of Theobald II Count of Champagne. This marriage strengthened the ties between the County of Tripoli and the Crusader states as well as Western Europe. Raymond III became the Count of Tripoli after his uncle Raymond II died childless in 1187. His inheritance was not immediately accepted by all factions particularly the Knights Templar and Hospitallers who had their own agendas and interests.\n\nRaymond III faced immediate challenges upon taking the throne. Saladin had been consolidating his power and the Battle of Hattin in 1187 resulted in a crushing defeat for the Crusader forces. This led to the fall of Jerusalem and many other strongholds. Raymond III however managed to hold Tripoli which remained under Crusader control. His ability to defend Tripoli against Saladin’s forces is considered one of his most notable achievements.\n\n*highlighted section part 2: First Tenure as Count*\nRaymond III’s first tenure as Count lasted two years from 1187 to 1189. During this time he worked to fortify Tripoli and maintain its defenses against Saladin. He also engaged in diplomatic efforts to secure alliances and support from other Crusader states and Western European powers. Despite these efforts the political and military situation in the Levant remained volatile. In 1189 Raymond III was forced to flee Tripoli temporarily due to internal conflicts and the pressure from Saladin’s armies. He traveled to Europe seeking reinforcements and diplomatic support but was unsuccessful in securing significant aid for his cause.\n\nIn 1197 after the death of Bohemond IV Prince of Antioch Raymond III resumed his rule over the County of Tripoli. This second period marked the latter part of his life and reign. By this time the Crusader states were much weakened and the region was dominated by Muslim forces but Raymond III continued his strategic and diplomatic efforts to protect Tripoli.\n\n*highlighted section part 3: Later Life and Legacy*\nDuring his second tenure Raymond III focused on the administration of the county and the defense of Tripoli. He maintained the city’s fortifications and oversaw its governance ensuring the stability of the region as much as possible. His reign was relatively peaceful compared to the turbulent years that followed his initial succession. Raymond III’s efforts to keep the County of Tripoli independent and secure were crucial in preserving one of the last remaining Crusader states in the Levant. He died in 1202 and was succeeded by his son Raymond-Roupen.\n\nRaymond III’s legacy is notable for his ability to navigate the complex political and military landscape of the Crusader states during a period of significant decline. His defensive strategies and diplomatic skills helped maintain Tripoli’s autonomy despite the overwhelming power of the Ayyubid dynasty. He represented the resilience of the Crusader states in the face of adversity and his reign is remembered as a pivotal time in the history of the County of Tripoli.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.06853077